SRC/ioccc/OSK -- three 1990 contest entries, as built for OS-9/68000====================================================================The twelve entries one directory up are as the contest published them, andqueens is built from baruch.c there unchanged.  These are copies of threemore, with the smallest changes that let Microware's C build them.  Eachchange below is every line that differs; `diff' the pair to see them.  bjack.c    NEW.  cmills.hin's compile notes pass four definitions with             -D: M=500 (largest bet), N=52 (one deck), X seeds rand()             from the clock, V is void or int.  This file supplies them             (V=int) and includes cmills.c.  cmills.c   `#include<stdio.h>' becomes `#include <stdio.h>', and the same             for time.h: Microware's cpp rejects the form with no space.  jaw.c      The include gets its space, as above.             `#define P putchar' becomes `#define P(x) fputc((x),stdout)'.             Microware's cpp does not join `P' to the `(' that follows it             in the source ("macro arguments required"); and stdio's             putchar macro evaluates its argument twice when output is             line-buffered, so `P(*--p)' popped two characters whenever             jaw wrote to a pipe or a terminal.  To a file it was right.             The table t and the arrays D and h are declared `remote':             they are 400K of static data, past the 64K the compiler             reaches directly ("value out of range").             The two 10s meaning end of line become '\n', which is CR here;             as 10, atob read the whole input looking for a line end.  scjones.c  The trigraphs are replaced by the characters they stand for,             which is what the contest's own common.mk does with sed for             a compiler without them, and the includes get their space.             What the program PRINTS is unchanged: the original entry,             trigraphs and all.  stdlib.h   EXIT_SUCCESS, EXIT_FAILURE and exit(), as common.mk writes             them.The programs: bjack is cmills.c (blackjack); jaw is jaw.c (zcat forcompress output, and atob when it runs under a name beginning with `a');trigraph is scjones.c (it prints its own source).